# r_cov_Pyykko.dat -- molecular single- and double-bond covalent radii, Pyykko & Atsumi (2009).
#
# CITE:  Pyykko, P. & Atsumi, M. (2009a). Molecular single-bond covalent radii
#        for elements 1-118. Chem. Eur. J. 15, 186-197.
#        doi:10.1002/chem.200800987 -- r1, Fig. 2, p. 192.
#        Pyykko, P. & Atsumi, M. (2009b). Molecular double-bond covalent radii
#        for elements Li-E112. Chem. Eur. J. 15, 12770-12779.
#        doi:10.1002/chem.200901472 -- r2, Fig. 3, p. 12775.
#
# STATUS: every number below is Pyykko & Atsumi's, transcribed for this
# package from the two printed figures; no file of anyone else's was copied.
# Fig. 3 carries the line "This table copyright (c) 2009 by Pekka Pyykko";
# only the numbers are reproduced here, never the figure. Cite the articles.
#
# WHAT THEY ARE: additive radii, R(AB) = r(A) + r(B), fitted self-consistently
# to experimental and theoretical E-E, E-H and E-CH3 bond lengths at the
# group valencies the papers choose (sigma = 2.8 pm over 410 points for r1,
# 3 pm over 316 for r2). They are radii for COVALENT bonds in molecules and
# molecular fragments (rigid bodies), not for extended inorganic solids, and
# not this package's bond table, which uses Cordero et al. (2008) through
# gemmi (model/geometry.py).
#
# TRANSCRIPTION (2026-10-02), read from the figure images at native
# resolution (both figures are rasters, so the PDFs hold no text for them).
#   r1: 118 values. The 2009b Fig. 3 reprint of r1 agrees on all 118, and so
#       do Wikipedia's "Covalent radius" Pyykko table and a machine OCR of
#       Fig. 2.
#   r2: 108 values; nan where the figure prints "-" or nothing (H, He, Fm,
#       No, E113-E118). Wikipedia agrees on all 108.
# The papers print E112-E118; they are written here with their IUPAC symbols
# (Cn, Nh, Fl, Mc, Lv, Ts, Og). r2 > r1 for Ne, Na, Ar, Cu, Zn, Kr, Ag, Cd,
# Xe, Hg, Rn and Cn, as printed (2009b, "Apparent anomalies", p. 12776).
#
# columns: symbol  Z  r1_pm  r2_pm  cn  (cn: the "typical coordination numbers
# assumed" row of Fig. 2, as printed; the f-block row is printed once beneath
# both the lanthanide and actinide rows and is applied to both here)

